46. CI, deployment, and governance

Keep one Pages deployment workflow.

Required proof order:
syntax
→ authoritative field, operation, scope, durable-object, normalizer, derivation, and ID registries
→ contract-profile migration
→ Stage 01 raw-intake, unitization, human-authority, and semantic-challenge tests
→ Stage 02 bounded-search and Stage 03 omission-challenge tests
→ Stage 04 obligation and challenge tests
→ Test IR language, dataflow, registry, parser, and security tests
→ deterministic runtime
→ file-first response ingestion and attachment-slot tests
→ workflow, due-stage, evidence, release, and terminal gates
→ prompt semantics, prompt-file identity, disclosure, and leakage tests
→ full-cycle and isolated mutation verification
→ local Chromium
→ deploy
→ exact deployed-origin and deployed-byte verification
→ deployed Chromium
→ actual physical iPhone Safari acceptance
→ final acceptance publication
→ release tag

Only main deploys.
A pull request cannot trigger a redundant production deployment.

A release tag is created only after:
- tests pass;
- deployment succeeds;
- live bytes are verified;
- deployed browser acceptance succeeds;
- actual physical iPhone Safari acceptance succeeds.

Repository governance requires the PR test job before merge.
If branch protection cannot be read, report it as unproven rather than assuming compliance.

The machine acceptance artifact identifies:
- exact commit;
- workflow identity;
- project schema;
- response schema;
- Test IR schema;
- ownership coverage;
- derivation coverage;
- relationship coverage;
- Stage 01 raw-input accounting;
- Stage 01 semantic mapping coverage;
- Stage 04 obligation coverage;
- accepted agent-value extraction coverage;
- current-scope selector coverage;
- native execution coverage;
- completed stages;
- negative cases;
- storage-failure cases;
- artifact round trip;
- deployed-byte identity;
- tested browser widths;
- live verification;
- MOBILE_ACCEPTANCE_TARGET_ID;
- physical iPhone device/iOS/Safari evidence;
- physical iPhone acceptance result and evidence ID.

46.1 Deployment origin identity

The deployment manifest binds canonical origin `https://sjonesjones917.github.io`, host `sjonesjones917.github.io`, base path `/closed-loop-tracker/`, the GitHub Pages production environment, a no-cross-origin-redirect policy, and the permitted same-origin runtime-resource set. Deployed verification rejects HTTP downgrade, unexpected host, unexpected custom domain, redirect outside policy, base-path mismatch, cross-origin substitution, or runtime resource outside the authorized deployment root. Identical bytes served from another origin do not satisfy the authorized deployment identity.

46.2 Specification, requirement-trace, and mobile evidence

The final machine acceptance artifact additionally identifies:
- repository-only specification-manifest filename and SHA-256;
- exact source commit and specification path;
- normative-requirement-manifest identity and digest;
- contract-profile identity;
- field, operation, scope, durable-object, normalizer, derivation, and ID registry identities;
- Test IR language and operation-registry identities;
- file-first prompt and response byte-identity coverage;
- attachment-slot coverage;
- due-stage and empty-universe metric coverage;
- terminal command and delivery-scope coverage;
- canonical deployment origin and manifest;
- MOBILE_ACCEPTANCE_TARGET_ID;
- physical iPhone model or hardware class, iOS build, Safari/WebKit identity where observable, viewport, test project, performer, exact deployed build, result, evidence IDs, and epistemic basis.

The repository-only specification and normative-requirement manifests remain CI evidence. They are not copied into ordinary runtime project state or external project prompts.

