11. Fixed product boundaries

The following remain unchanged unless a demonstrated failing case requires a narrowly justified correction:
- exactly 30 stages;
- workflow identity mobile-closed-loop/30;
- one canonical project model;
- one prompt authority;
- one response-ingestion path;
- one workflow engine;
- one project store;
- one application shell;
- provider-neutral external-agent protocol;
- raw-first processing;
- deterministic canonical IDs;
- conservative downstream invalidation;
- exactly ten runs;
- current artifact-store concept;
- release stages 27-29;
- permanent defect history;
- browser-local static deployment;
- no backend synchronization;
- no provider-specific adapters;
- no adaptive run count;
- no risk-based stage skipping;
- no domain-mode architecture;
- no plugin framework;
- no general event-sourcing rewrite;
- no framework rewrite;
- no runtime prompt-wrapper rewriting;
- no MutationObserver patch;
- no monkey patch;
- no redundant parser family;
- no second project store;
- no second workflow engine;
- no second application shell;
- no Stage 31;
- no Operation 31.

The Test IR requirement authorizes only the narrow additional runtime components needed for deterministic test execution.

The fixed external-handoff boundary is file-first: one authoritative prompt file, one manifest-bound input set, one authoritative response JSON file, and zero or more slot-bound returned files. Text previews and clipboard actions are not canonical transports.

⸻

12. Target architecture and responsible files

Responsibility → final owner:
- Stage identities, names, roles, declared completion conditions → workbook.js
- Field ownership, types, enums, relationships, stage contracts, Test IR response shape → workflow-schema.js
- Canonical serialization and SHA-256 → hash.js
- Test IR registry, validation support, runtime capabilities, worker coordination → test-runtime.js
- Isolated Test IR execution → test-worker.js
- Derived state, current scope, gates, routing, handoff, independence, evidence sufficiency, contradictions, stability, invalidation, convergence, release → workflow-engine.js
- Prompt content, exact context selection, withheld context, blind aliases, prompt identity → prompt-engine.js
- Parsing, closed-schema validation, proposal planning, stale detection, external-result ingestion → response-ingestion.js
- Revisions, actual artifact bytes, migration, integrity, import/export, artifact download, execution packages → project-store.js
- Rendering, operator actions, native-test command initiation, accessibility behavior → app-core.js
- Static shell, CSS, CSP, script loading, live regions → index.html
- Syntax, schema, runtime, ingestion, workflow, prompt, browser, deployment, and live proof → existing verification files and .github/workflows/pages.yml

12.1 Runtime script order

The runtime dependency order is:
1. workbook.js
2. hash.js
3. workflow-schema.js
4. test-runtime.js
5. workflow-engine.js
6. prompt-engine.js
7. response-ingestion.js
8. project-store.js
9. app-core.js

test-worker.js is loaded only as the same-origin worker entry used by test-runtime.js.
The complete runtime graph, including the worker URL, MUST use one shared build/cache identity.
app.js and document.write() MUST NOT be runtime loading mechanisms.
One-time repair files must be removed after their behavior is integrated and regression-proven.
Active diagnostics, migrations, acceptance tests, deployment verification, and live-verification infrastructure must remain.

12.2 CSP

The CSP must remain restrictive.
It may permit only the same-origin worker required for test-worker.js.
It must not enable arbitrary:
- scripts;
- workers;
- network access;
- dynamic evaluation;
- inline code execution;
- third-party execution.

12.3 Additional responsibility boundaries

workflow-schema.js owns the authoritative field registry, stage-operation registry, scope matrix, durable-object contracts, attachment-slot contract, verification-phase fields, and date/time value schemas.

test-runtime.js owns the Test IR language identity, operation-registry identity and digest, explicit DAG dataflow validation, parser and selector registry identities, and deterministic error propagation.

workflow-engine.js owns due-stage evaluation, release-state precedence, external-action risk evaluation, reservation state transitions, delivery-candidate closure, terminal prerequisite calculation, and the distinction between authorization, attempted delivery, and observed delivery.

project-store.js owns file-first prompt/response bytes, staging recovery, idempotency and tombstone receipts, backup/export state, logical handoff-container membership, quarantine handling, and exact original-byte preservation.

app-core.js renders untrusted values only through safe text and attribute APIs, applies the closed URL policy, exposes file-first actions, performs human confirmation without retyping, and presents actual-iPhone operator actions.

Repository CI owns the repository-only specification manifest and normative-requirement manifest. Those governance artifacts are not loaded into runtime project state or external project prompts.

⸻

13. Versioning and migration

Required identities:
WORKFLOW_ID      = mobile-closed-loop/30
PROJECT_SCHEMA   = closed-loop-project/3
RESPONSE_SCHEMA  = closed-loop-stage-response/3
TEST_IR_SCHEMA   = closed-loop-test-spec/1
PACKAGE_SCHEMA   = closed-loop-verification-package/1
STAGE_COUNT      = 30
CONTRACT_PROFILE = closed-loop-completion-profile/1

The following versioned contract identities defined elsewhere in this specification are equally controlling and version-bound under Section 13.2: closed-loop-canonical-json/1, closed-loop-id/1, closed-loop-filename/1, closed-loop-handoff-container/1, closed-loop-specification-manifest/1, closed-loop-normative-requirements/1, closed-loop-deployment-manifest/1, closed-loop-encrypted-export/1, closed-loop-test-ir-language/1, closed-loop-test-ir-operations/1, closed-loop-json-selector/1, closed-loop-xml-selector/1, closed-loop-regex/1, closed-loop-trusted-time/1, closed-loop-raw-units/1, closed-loop-archive-profile/1 where archives are used, SEMANTIC_CHALLENGE_LIMITS/1, PROMPT_INLINE_LIMITS/1, and every parser, selector, regex, normalizer, and derivation registry identity.

Required deterministic migration:
human-project/30
→ closed-loop-project/2
→ closed-loop-project/3

A direct legacy-to-/3 migration is permitted only when it produces the same canonical result and audit history.

Migration MUST preserve:
- unknown extension data;
- raw responses;
- validation results;
- pending proposals;
- receipts;
- extraction manifests;
- Stage 01 raw-input and semantic intake metadata;
- Stage 04 obligation manifests;
- prompt/context manifests;
- historical records;
- project identity;
- artifact identity;
- actual artifact bytes;
- all 30 stages;
- append-only history;
- original imported payload as non-operational audit evidence.

Old /2 raw responses remain historical.
They MUST NOT satisfy a current /3 prompt or contract.
Imported legacy stage records, nested original projects, recovery payloads, or duplicate state must not become competing active canonical state or satisfy current gates.

13.1 Contract-profile migration

Every current /3 project carries CONTRACT_PROFILE_ID = closed-loop-completion-profile/1. A /3 project lacking that exact profile is pre-profile legacy data and cannot satisfy current gates. Migration either deterministically creates every required current-profile record and field with auditable receipts and valid provenance or preserves the project as historical and non-gating. Merely setting CONTRACT_PROFILE_ID without satisfying the profile validator is rejected.

Migration defaults cannot fabricate semantic review, human authority, execution, evidence, freshness, independence, backup custody, mobile acceptance, or delivery. Any such missing release-bearing fact remains UNKNOWN or incomplete. Contract-profile migration is idempotent and is covered by invalid legacy fixtures.

13.2 Registry-version migration

Every persisted reference to a field contract, stage operation, scope matrix, Test IR language or operation registry, parser or selector registry, canonicalization contract, ID contract, filename contract, date/time contract, handoff container, normalizer, or derivation is version-bound. Import or migration must either prove compatibility under an explicit migration rule or invalidate and regenerate the affected current work. Equal high-level schema names do not imply registry compatibility.

